1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,060
Good morning, everybody.

2
00:00:02,540 --> 00:00:03,420
Yeah, good morning.

3
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:06,400
So thank you for attending our presentation.

4
00:00:07,160 --> 00:00:10,900
So I'm Milene, and with my colleague, Quentin,

5
00:00:11,240 --> 00:00:13,300
we will present you the secure boot.

6
00:00:15,900 --> 00:00:17,320
A short presentation.

7
00:00:17,760 --> 00:00:20,700
So we are embedded Linux engineer at Bootlin,

8
00:00:21,080 --> 00:00:23,120
formerly Free Electrons,

9
00:00:23,540 --> 00:00:27,320
but due to some trademark dispute,

10
00:00:27,320 --> 00:00:29,460
we changed our company name,

11
00:00:29,460 --> 00:00:35,060
but that's it. We just changed the company name. We are still the same engineers, the same team,

12
00:00:35,060 --> 00:00:43,620
and still we have a strong open source focus. For our customer, we implemented a full chain

13
00:00:43,620 --> 00:00:53,220
of trust on a custom IMX6 board, and we wanted to share it in case it can help other people. So

14
00:00:53,220 --> 00:00:58,220
So this is what we are going to talk in this presentation.

15
00:01:01,100 --> 00:01:05,440
Short disclaimer, we are definitely not a security expert.

16
00:01:06,340 --> 00:01:11,340
And we are presenting you one way to verify both

17
00:01:11,800 --> 00:01:16,220
on a board based on a specific family of SOCs,

18
00:01:16,220 --> 00:01:21,220
but some parts can be applied to other boards.

19
00:01:21,220 --> 00:01:31,700
And a container will tell you when there is some specificity on IAMX SOC.

20
00:01:31,700 --> 00:01:41,220
So first, in introduction, who wants to verify the boot sequence and why?

21
00:01:41,220 --> 00:01:43,500
So there is product vendors.

22
00:01:43,500 --> 00:01:49,780
They want to make sure that the devices are used in the same way they should be and not

23
00:01:49,780 --> 00:01:57,780
for a different purpose. Also not for running inappropriate software. So for

24
00:01:57,780 --> 00:02:08,760
example, a company may have some software limitation and would need license for

25
00:02:08,760 --> 00:02:15,400
customer to have a fully featured software. So it can be used with the

26
00:02:15,400 --> 00:02:17,940
chain of trust to be sure that the user

27
00:02:17,940 --> 00:02:23,720
are using the fully-featured software with a license.

28
00:02:23,720 --> 00:02:27,900
And of course, to protect customers

29
00:02:27,900 --> 00:02:32,680
without having some malwares on your system.

30
00:02:32,680 --> 00:02:34,460
There is also end users.

31
00:02:34,460 --> 00:02:37,980
They may want to make sure that the system has not

32
00:02:37,980 --> 00:02:40,500
been tampered with.

33
00:02:40,500 --> 00:02:44,520
So basically, it's to make sure that the binaries you are

34
00:02:44,520 --> 00:02:53,700
trying to load, boot, or execute by a person that you trust.

35
00:02:53,700 --> 00:02:56,460
So how does it work?

36
00:02:56,460 --> 00:03:00,720
Everything is based on digital signature verification, which

37
00:03:00,720 --> 00:03:03,180
is different from encryption.

38
00:03:03,180 --> 00:03:06,080
So Quentin will show you in the next slides

39
00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:11,940
the difference between signature verification and encryption.

40
00:03:11,940 --> 00:03:15,440
So in fact, the first element in the boot process

41
00:03:15,440 --> 00:03:17,560
authenticate the second one.

42
00:03:17,560 --> 00:03:20,280
The second one will authenticate the third one,

43
00:03:20,280 --> 00:03:21,420
et cetera, et cetera.

44
00:03:21,420 --> 00:03:24,720
And that's why it's called a chain of trust,

45
00:03:24,720 --> 00:03:28,680
because you have this chain mechanism.

46
00:03:28,680 --> 00:03:32,400
And if any element is authenticated,

47
00:03:32,400 --> 00:03:36,560
but not sufficiently locked down, so for example,

48
00:03:36,560 --> 00:03:40,260
if you let console access in bootloader

49
00:03:40,260 --> 00:03:46,700
or with access in user space, the device is not verified anymore.

50
00:03:46,700 --> 00:03:49,620
And so you will break the chain of trust.

51
00:03:53,400 --> 00:03:59,300
So this is how the chain of trust looks like.

52
00:03:59,300 --> 00:04:01,580
So you have the ROM code.

53
00:04:01,580 --> 00:04:03,940
It has a public key.

54
00:04:03,940 --> 00:04:08,580
It uses this public key to perform a signature verification

55
00:04:08,580 --> 00:04:10,100
on the bootloader.

56
00:04:10,100 --> 00:04:13,220
Then the root loader have a public key

57
00:04:13,220 --> 00:04:17,880
and it will perform signature verification on the kernel.

58
00:04:17,880 --> 00:04:19,860
And the kernel will have a digest

59
00:04:19,860 --> 00:04:23,880
to perform a hash verification on the root file system.

60
00:04:25,080 --> 00:04:27,580
So every component is verified using

61
00:04:27,580 --> 00:04:31,340
its digital signature and a public key.

62
00:04:32,340 --> 00:04:34,140
The root file system integrity is verified

63
00:04:34,140 --> 00:04:35,640
using a hash mechanism.

64
00:04:35,640 --> 00:04:40,640
We'll see later in next slides how it works.

65
00:04:40,640 --> 00:04:43,800
And our experience, so as I said before,

66
00:04:43,800 --> 00:04:47,960
we implemented it on a custom IAMX6 board.

67
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:51,960
Content work on the chain of trust

68
00:04:51,960 --> 00:04:55,160
from room code to the kernel,

69
00:04:55,160 --> 00:04:58,020
and I worked on the root file system parts.

70
00:04:59,620 --> 00:05:04,680
Okay, so now a little bit of, well,

71
00:05:04,680 --> 00:05:07,420
explanation on what are the differences between encryption

72
00:05:07,420 --> 00:05:08,680
and signature.

73
00:05:08,680 --> 00:05:12,740
So as Milan told you, the verified boot sequence,

74
00:05:12,740 --> 00:05:15,460
or a secure boot, as it's called,

75
00:05:15,460 --> 00:05:18,180
is based on signature and not encryption.

76
00:05:18,180 --> 00:05:21,680
These are different things, but they are compatible.

77
00:05:21,680 --> 00:05:25,540
You can actually have encrypted data that is also signed.

78
00:05:25,540 --> 00:05:30,060
So here is the monetary Alice and Bob example about encryption.

79
00:05:30,060 --> 00:05:33,600
So the point of encryption is to be

80
00:05:33,600 --> 00:05:40,780
able to send some data that is unreadable for anyone else

81
00:05:40,780 --> 00:05:43,480
that isn't supposed to receive, for example, the mail.

82
00:05:43,480 --> 00:05:46,980
So here, we want to be sure that the mail Alice is sending

83
00:05:46,980 --> 00:05:52,720
to Bob when it can only be read by Bob.

84
00:05:52,720 --> 00:05:56,880
So you have as input the data and the public key of Bob,

85
00:05:56,880 --> 00:05:59,640
which is the input to a math function.

86
00:05:59,640 --> 00:06:03,300
So we don't care what is it, but it's math function.

87
00:06:03,300 --> 00:06:06,180
And then it outputs the encrypted data.

88
00:06:06,180 --> 00:06:07,500
You send it over the internet.

89
00:06:07,500 --> 00:06:10,000
Nobody can read it except Bob.

90
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:13,500
Bob receives the encrypted data, and it

91
00:06:13,500 --> 00:06:20,360
put it into a math function along with his private key,

92
00:06:20,360 --> 00:06:23,120
which is the only one to own.

93
00:06:23,120 --> 00:06:25,980
Of course, it's his private key.

94
00:06:25,980 --> 00:06:29,160
into my function, and then you have the decrypted data.

95
00:06:29,160 --> 00:06:31,500
And you're sure Bob is the only one

96
00:06:31,500 --> 00:06:33,920
who is able to read this data.

97
00:06:33,920 --> 00:06:38,140
The next one is actually the signature verification.

98
00:06:38,140 --> 00:06:41,280
So here is a different purpose.

99
00:06:41,280 --> 00:06:44,160
Here, you want to be sure that Alice,

100
00:06:44,160 --> 00:06:47,980
so the one who sent the mail to Bob is actually Alice

101
00:06:47,980 --> 00:06:50,440
and not somebody else.

102
00:06:50,440 --> 00:06:54,860
The data is unencrypted, so everybody can read it.

103
00:06:54,860 --> 00:07:01,040
So basically, Alice will take her private key and her data,

104
00:07:01,040 --> 00:07:02,900
put it into a math function.

105
00:07:02,900 --> 00:07:04,500
It will output assigned data.

106
00:07:04,500 --> 00:07:06,760
It's readable by anyone.

107
00:07:06,760 --> 00:07:09,440
It's a very important concept.

108
00:07:09,440 --> 00:07:11,540
Everybody can read it.

109
00:07:11,540 --> 00:07:15,420
And go through the internet, and Bob receives it.

110
00:07:15,420 --> 00:07:16,460
He can read it.

111
00:07:16,460 --> 00:07:21,040
And to attest that Alice is the one who sent the mail,

112
00:07:21,040 --> 00:07:23,420
Well, it will use the public key of Alice

113
00:07:23,420 --> 00:07:27,300
to actually verify the signature.

114
00:07:27,300 --> 00:07:29,720
Goes into a math function again, a different one,

115
00:07:29,720 --> 00:07:32,500
and, well, the data is verified or not.

116
00:07:32,500 --> 00:07:36,700
So we can attest it's actually Alice who has sent the mail.

117
00:07:36,700 --> 00:07:39,240
So that's it for the differences between encryption

118
00:07:39,240 --> 00:07:40,920
and signature.

119
00:07:40,920 --> 00:07:47,340
So now you have to know that, well, the secure boot sequence,

120
00:07:47,340 --> 00:07:49,720
it has consequences, of course.

121
00:07:49,720 --> 00:07:54,880
It's costly in terms of setting up

122
00:07:54,880 --> 00:07:57,640
the whole architecture for managing your keys,

123
00:07:57,640 --> 00:08:01,360
creating your keys, signing all your binaries, et cetera.

124
00:08:01,360 --> 00:08:07,780
So of course, it's a bit longer to handle all those cases.

125
00:08:07,780 --> 00:08:10,740
You have some added complexity to your workflow

126
00:08:10,740 --> 00:08:14,480
for developers, because once your platform is locked down

127
00:08:14,480 --> 00:08:18,000
and can only boot verified binaries,

128
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:22,900
you have to sign those binaries to test them.

129
00:08:22,900 --> 00:08:24,960
And of course, the boot time is, well,

130
00:08:24,960 --> 00:08:28,200
increased because, well, you have a bunch of authentication

131
00:08:28,200 --> 00:08:33,120
along the way to the prompt boot, to the Linux prompt, sorry.

132
00:08:33,120 --> 00:08:36,060
And well, last but not least, you really

133
00:08:36,060 --> 00:08:37,920
have to be careful with your private keys

134
00:08:37,920 --> 00:08:40,800
because if one gets stolen or leaked,

135
00:08:40,800 --> 00:08:42,660
then, well, you're pretty much screwed up

136
00:08:42,660 --> 00:08:46,760
because, well, anyone can sign your binaries

137
00:08:46,760 --> 00:08:49,400
and then it's not secure anymore.

138
00:08:49,400 --> 00:08:51,220
So that was it for the introduction.

139
00:08:51,220 --> 00:08:53,520
And now we can go to the first element of the chain

140
00:08:53,520 --> 00:08:57,920
of trust, the RAM card, or as it's called, the root of trust.

141
00:08:57,920 --> 00:09:03,420
So this is really the main point of your chain of trust.

142
00:09:03,420 --> 00:09:05,720
It's very specific to the SOC.

143
00:09:05,720 --> 00:09:07,440
So it depends on your vendors.

144
00:09:07,440 --> 00:09:11,340
Not all SOCs support secure booting.

145
00:09:11,340 --> 00:09:14,840
And well, as I told you before, you

146
00:09:14,840 --> 00:09:17,880
need the public key to be able to verify the signature

147
00:09:17,880 --> 00:09:19,100
of binary.

148
00:09:19,100 --> 00:09:22,380
So you need a way to securely store it somewhere

149
00:09:22,380 --> 00:09:24,580
that the ROM code can look at.

150
00:09:24,580 --> 00:09:30,080
And well, that depends on every vendor implementation.

151
00:09:30,080 --> 00:09:33,900
And it's definitely embedded in the ROM code,

152
00:09:33,900 --> 00:09:35,300
so you don't have access to it.

153
00:09:35,300 --> 00:09:37,280
You have to trace your vendor.

154
00:09:37,280 --> 00:09:39,640
And there are definitely different vendors that

155
00:09:39,640 --> 00:09:41,560
implement some kind of secure boot.

156
00:09:41,560 --> 00:09:44,240
So these are the main one, I would say.

157
00:09:44,240 --> 00:09:50,260
Xilinx, Tegra, NXP, et cetera, and missing some shortly.

158
00:09:50,260 --> 00:09:54,480
So now you have to have a way to store your public keys.

159
00:09:54,480 --> 00:09:58,720
So this is very the specific way for NXP

160
00:09:58,720 --> 00:10:00,860
to handle the public keys.

161
00:10:00,860 --> 00:10:04,820
So basically, it's a non-volatile memory, of course,

162
00:10:04,820 --> 00:10:10,160
because you need it to be up when the bootstrum starts.

163
00:10:10,160 --> 00:10:14,100
NXP uses OTP, so one-time programmable fuses.

164
00:10:14,100 --> 00:10:22,920
So basically a fuse, eFuse also, is, well, we could illustrate it with a wire.

165
00:10:22,920 --> 00:10:27,940
And when the current path through the wire, then it's a binary one.

166
00:10:27,940 --> 00:10:32,840
And you apply a very, very, very high current to it, and it disrupts the wire.

167
00:10:32,840 --> 00:10:35,140
And then you're going to get a binary zero.

168
00:10:35,140 --> 00:10:36,620
That's how it works.

169
00:10:36,620 --> 00:10:38,460
And it's irreversible.

170
00:10:38,460 --> 00:10:43,200
So once you have blown a fuse, then, well,

171
00:10:43,200 --> 00:10:45,900
never gets wired again.

172
00:10:45,900 --> 00:10:48,280
Otifix uses are really, really expensive

173
00:10:48,280 --> 00:10:51,900
in terms of size occupied on your PCB.

174
00:10:51,900 --> 00:10:56,400
And whereas the public is already at least 1 kilobyte,

175
00:10:56,400 --> 00:11:00,600
and sometimes up to 4 kilobytes,

176
00:11:00,600 --> 00:11:03,900
so maybe a good idea would be to actually store

177
00:11:03,900 --> 00:11:07,500
the hash of this key, which is way, way smaller,

178
00:11:07,500 --> 00:11:13,620
into the OTP and have the public key in another place.

179
00:11:13,620 --> 00:11:16,660
For example, in the binary, you're going to load.

180
00:11:16,660 --> 00:11:19,680
That way, you just have to check the hash of the public key

181
00:11:19,680 --> 00:11:21,940
that is embedded somewhere else.

182
00:11:21,940 --> 00:11:25,380
And if it matches, then you're good to go.

183
00:11:25,380 --> 00:11:28,080
Well, now we have plenty of space,

184
00:11:28,080 --> 00:11:32,980
so why not using maybe four keys so that we can actually

185
00:11:32,980 --> 00:11:38,160
revoke some if some get stolen or, well, lost.

186
00:11:38,160 --> 00:11:42,600
So that way, you can just revoke one key and just say,

187
00:11:42,600 --> 00:11:44,680
well, this one, we don't want to use it anymore.

188
00:11:44,680 --> 00:11:49,080
We can use any of the three remaining ones.

189
00:11:49,080 --> 00:11:51,560
So that's it.

190
00:11:51,560 --> 00:11:54,440
For the next slide, yeah, thank you.

191
00:11:54,440 --> 00:11:57,380
The ROM code, so basically the process

192
00:11:57,380 --> 00:12:01,660
is to load the bootloader in a secure space

193
00:12:01,660 --> 00:12:03,440
to avoid physical attacks.

194
00:12:03,440 --> 00:12:07,740
So the point is here, if you load your bootloader

195
00:12:07,740 --> 00:12:11,440
and authenticate it from a space that is unsecure,

196
00:12:11,440 --> 00:12:15,160
the problem is, once you have to actually boot,

197
00:12:15,160 --> 00:12:19,520
execute the binary, well, this is a time frame

198
00:12:19,520 --> 00:12:22,140
where an attacker can actually change the binary

199
00:12:22,140 --> 00:12:25,560
and make it like it was authenticated

200
00:12:25,560 --> 00:12:29,260
while it was definitely not.

201
00:12:29,260 --> 00:12:37,220
Then it loads the embedded public key, which is in the bootloader binary, for example.

202
00:12:37,220 --> 00:12:41,620
It checks the hash of the public key, which is embedded in the binary of the bootloader

203
00:12:41,620 --> 00:12:48,400
against the one which is in the hash table, made of different OTPs, fuses.

204
00:12:48,400 --> 00:12:53,880
You check the public key, then you execute the bootloader binary if it matches.

205
00:12:53,880 --> 00:13:05,380
And on NXP for IMX, et cetera, SOC family, it's called HAB for High Insurance Boot.

206
00:13:05,380 --> 00:13:08,260
So how do you set it up for your board?

207
00:13:08,260 --> 00:13:11,380
It's, again, very specific to your vendor.

208
00:13:11,380 --> 00:13:18,980
Here, you use code signing tools, so CST, for creating the keys, so the private keys

209
00:13:18,980 --> 00:13:20,860
and certificates.

210
00:13:20,860 --> 00:13:27,280
You flush the fuses using working and verified uBoot,

211
00:13:27,280 --> 00:13:31,480
again, using NXP-specific code, which is Fuse Flush or something

212
00:13:31,480 --> 00:13:35,020
like that, Fuse Prog, I think, with the Fuse table written

213
00:13:35,020 --> 00:13:36,620
by CST.

214
00:13:36,620 --> 00:13:39,320
You sign the bootloader using, again, CST,

215
00:13:39,320 --> 00:13:41,620
and you check the status of the bootloader,

216
00:13:41,620 --> 00:13:44,620
H-I-B underscore status, from the bootloader,

217
00:13:44,620 --> 00:13:49,500
so from uBoot, with a signed uBoot, which is also NXP-specific.

218
00:13:49,500 --> 00:13:53,100
And if you have reached this step successfully,

219
00:13:53,100 --> 00:13:55,260
then you can decide to lock down your bootloader

220
00:13:55,260 --> 00:13:59,820
to actually restrict the loading of the bootloader to one

221
00:13:59,820 --> 00:14:02,980
which is authenticated, so verified.

222
00:14:02,980 --> 00:14:07,200
And well, if you just screwed one of the previous item

223
00:14:07,200 --> 00:14:10,180
and you blow the locking fuse, then you screwed up.

224
00:14:10,180 --> 00:14:14,940
So be sure you are doing the right thing.

225
00:14:14,940 --> 00:14:19,620
So this is the two examples of the results of HAB status

226
00:14:19,620 --> 00:14:21,060
from your boot.

227
00:14:21,060 --> 00:14:24,140
The first example, you have the first line,

228
00:14:24,140 --> 00:14:25,420
which is secure boot disabled.

229
00:14:25,420 --> 00:14:28,820
This is because I haven't blown, actually, the locking fuse.

230
00:14:28,820 --> 00:14:31,440
When it's blown, then it's secure boot enabled.

231
00:14:31,440 --> 00:14:35,000
If you have any HAB event following this line,

232
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:38,560
it means that your bootloader hasn't been authenticated.

233
00:14:38,560 --> 00:14:40,540
So it failed.

234
00:14:40,540 --> 00:14:44,020
In the next example, you can see no HAB events found.

235
00:14:44,020 --> 00:14:47,260
well, you successfully authenticated your bootloader.

236
00:14:47,260 --> 00:14:49,800
So that was it for the ROM code part,

237
00:14:49,800 --> 00:14:52,660
to authenticate the bootloader.

238
00:14:52,660 --> 00:14:56,480
But now you have to authenticate your kernel.

239
00:14:56,480 --> 00:14:59,340
So trust no one.

240
00:14:59,340 --> 00:15:00,740
Believe me, trust no one.

241
00:15:00,740 --> 00:15:03,380
So there is no point of having a secure bootloader

242
00:15:03,380 --> 00:15:06,840
if it's not authenticated by the ROM code.

243
00:15:06,840 --> 00:15:09,820
So by that, I mean if you can actually

244
00:15:09,820 --> 00:15:14,080
switch the bootloader with something that isn't

245
00:15:14,080 --> 00:15:18,960
authenticated, then there is no point actually

246
00:15:18,960 --> 00:15:20,740
taking the time to authenticate it.

247
00:15:20,740 --> 00:15:25,100
Because with a not-authenticated bootloader,

248
00:15:25,100 --> 00:15:28,580
you can boot whatever you want after that.

249
00:15:28,580 --> 00:15:30,820
The bootloader has to be sufficiently locked down.

250
00:15:30,820 --> 00:15:34,060
So it depends on the bootloader binary you're booting.

251
00:15:34,060 --> 00:15:38,460
And there is a very specific use case,

252
00:15:38,460 --> 00:15:41,820
Well, example for U-boot in mainline.

253
00:15:41,820 --> 00:15:45,560
U-boot in mainline has environment variable called

254
00:15:45,560 --> 00:15:46,860
verify.

255
00:15:46,860 --> 00:15:49,940
If it's set to no, U-boot stops to verify

256
00:15:49,940 --> 00:15:52,000
the signature of images.

257
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:56,140
And that's, well, not what we want.

258
00:15:56,140 --> 00:15:58,220
So we definitely do not want any user

259
00:15:58,220 --> 00:16:01,940
to access the console to set the environment variable from there.

260
00:16:01,940 --> 00:16:06,040
So you have to disable the console at all.

261
00:16:06,040 --> 00:16:08,620
Well, very important point.

262
00:16:08,620 --> 00:16:11,180
You should, under no circumstances,

263
00:16:11,180 --> 00:16:14,780
trust anything that isn't in an authenticated binary.

264
00:16:14,780 --> 00:16:17,180
So that means that, well, environments

265
00:16:17,180 --> 00:16:19,960
are often on other mediums.

266
00:16:19,960 --> 00:16:22,800
But there, you can trust them, because you cannot authenticate

267
00:16:22,800 --> 00:16:23,680
them.

268
00:16:23,680 --> 00:16:25,800
So you should use the one which is definitely

269
00:16:25,800 --> 00:16:28,520
in the you would binary that was authenticated by the ROM

270
00:16:28,520 --> 00:16:30,940
code.

271
00:16:30,940 --> 00:16:36,020
Well, that doesn't really work with update software

272
00:16:36,020 --> 00:16:40,800
from Linux kernel, because they often interact

273
00:16:40,800 --> 00:16:44,120
with the uBoot environments to say,

274
00:16:44,120 --> 00:16:47,320
you should boot this image or this image.

275
00:16:47,320 --> 00:16:49,960
So I have a pending patch for uBoot

276
00:16:49,960 --> 00:16:51,800
to only load a handful of variables

277
00:16:51,800 --> 00:16:59,260
that you can control from the uBoot embedded environment

278
00:16:59,260 --> 00:17:03,440
binary, and it can load from whichever medium

279
00:17:03,440 --> 00:17:07,540
you want to load the environment from.

280
00:17:07,540 --> 00:17:10,660
So yeah, that's it.

281
00:17:10,660 --> 00:17:12,820
So now, how it actually works, you're

282
00:17:12,820 --> 00:17:16,660
going to use the device through blob, which is basically

283
00:17:16,660 --> 00:17:20,060
the same as it's in the kernel.

284
00:17:20,060 --> 00:17:24,040
It's used to actually embed the public key

285
00:17:24,040 --> 00:17:26,660
within the uBoot binary.

286
00:17:26,660 --> 00:17:29,580
And this public key will be used to check the signature

287
00:17:29,580 --> 00:17:32,240
of the kernel images.

288
00:17:32,240 --> 00:17:35,680
And because it's appended to the UBoot binary,

289
00:17:35,680 --> 00:17:37,880
it's also authenticated by the ROM card.

290
00:17:37,880 --> 00:17:40,280
So you can actually trust the public key, which

291
00:17:40,280 --> 00:17:45,340
is within the UBoot binary.

292
00:17:45,340 --> 00:17:51,280
So now, to be a bit more practical and simple to use,

293
00:17:51,280 --> 00:17:54,760
to check the signature of the different images,

294
00:17:54,760 --> 00:17:55,700
you have two choices.

295
00:17:55,700 --> 00:17:58,560
Either you load one image, and then its signature,

296
00:17:58,560 --> 00:18:01,440
and then the second image, and its signature, et cetera,

297
00:18:01,440 --> 00:18:05,320
or you use the fit image, which is basically a container

298
00:18:05,320 --> 00:18:10,160
storing a lot of different binaries and their hashes.

299
00:18:10,160 --> 00:18:14,660
And MKImage, so the tool to actually compile the fit image,

300
00:18:14,660 --> 00:18:18,040
has already built-in support for signing in while doing

301
00:18:18,040 --> 00:18:19,100
the binaries hash.

302
00:18:19,100 --> 00:18:20,760
So well, we're on luck.

303
00:18:20,760 --> 00:18:23,260
So better use this tool.

304
00:18:23,260 --> 00:18:30,980
So well, now in the process to get to the next chain

305
00:18:30,980 --> 00:18:33,040
in a chain of trust, you have to create actually

306
00:18:33,040 --> 00:18:38,840
your keys to verify the kernel to actually sign the bootloader.

307
00:18:38,840 --> 00:18:39,340
Sorry.

308
00:18:39,340 --> 00:18:42,640
To sign the kernel and verify it from the bootloader.

309
00:18:42,640 --> 00:18:47,160
So the first line is to create an RSA 4K key, which

310
00:18:47,160 --> 00:18:49,080
is called my underscore key.

311
00:18:49,080 --> 00:18:50,180
This is a private key.

312
00:18:50,180 --> 00:18:51,860
And then you create a certificate.

313
00:18:51,860 --> 00:18:56,340
Certificates is really close to a public key.

314
00:18:56,340 --> 00:19:01,940
So it's really MKMitch requires the use of certificates

315
00:19:01,940 --> 00:19:03,320
and not public keys.

316
00:19:03,320 --> 00:19:04,900
Well, it's a choice.

317
00:19:04,900 --> 00:19:08,220
And it's really, really important that your certificate

318
00:19:08,220 --> 00:19:10,420
and your private key are named the same.

319
00:19:10,420 --> 00:19:12,600
Otherwise, MKMitch doesn't work at all.

320
00:19:15,920 --> 00:19:20,240
So this is an example of a source file for the device

321
00:19:20,240 --> 00:19:25,920
tree used to embed the public key within the UBoot binary.

322
00:19:25,920 --> 00:19:28,700
So you have the signature device 3 node.

323
00:19:28,700 --> 00:19:32,000
If your board already has device 3,

324
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:34,360
well, you should be able to use it and just add

325
00:19:34,360 --> 00:19:36,840
the signature device 3 node.

326
00:19:36,840 --> 00:19:40,480
Something to note is that key name hint and the suffix

327
00:19:40,480 --> 00:19:46,660
to key dash dt node has to be the same value or name

328
00:19:46,660 --> 00:19:48,040
as the one you have given to your key.

329
00:19:48,040 --> 00:19:50,420
So in the previous slide, it was my underscore key.

330
00:19:50,420 --> 00:19:53,020
You have to use the same.

331
00:19:53,020 --> 00:19:57,520
There is also a property, which is called required.

332
00:19:57,520 --> 00:20:00,640
It's either image or configuration.

333
00:20:00,640 --> 00:20:03,500
It depends on which level you want to actually

334
00:20:03,500 --> 00:20:06,620
do your signature verification.

335
00:20:06,620 --> 00:20:11,980
And you can find the explanation in the documentation here

336
00:20:11,980 --> 00:20:14,220
in the UBoot project.

337
00:20:14,220 --> 00:20:16,380
So what's a fit image?

338
00:20:16,380 --> 00:20:18,000
So let's say it's basically a container.

339
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:22,140
So you have a set of images, different one, one, two,

340
00:20:22,140 --> 00:20:24,400
three, kernel, whatever.

341
00:20:24,400 --> 00:20:26,840
The same for device trees, you have also

342
00:20:26,840 --> 00:20:31,100
support for other kind of files like scripts.

343
00:20:31,100 --> 00:20:33,440
And then you have also configurations.

344
00:20:33,440 --> 00:20:36,940
So configurations are a way to force the user

345
00:20:36,940 --> 00:20:40,920
to load binaries together.

346
00:20:40,920 --> 00:20:45,820
So for example, here you have conf at one, so the red box,

347
00:20:45,820 --> 00:20:48,240
which force the user to load the first kernel, the third

348
00:20:48,240 --> 00:20:51,800
device tree, and the first .

349
00:20:51,800 --> 00:20:54,560
So that's a way to force your user

350
00:20:54,560 --> 00:20:58,360
to boot a certain configuration.

351
00:20:58,360 --> 00:21:02,200
It supports different architectures, OSCs, image

352
00:21:02,200 --> 00:21:04,820
types, and a lot of other things.

353
00:21:04,820 --> 00:21:07,160
And you can find all of them in the source code

354
00:21:07,160 --> 00:21:10,660
in command slash image.c.

355
00:21:10,660 --> 00:21:14,580
So this is really a quick example of a source file

356
00:21:14,580 --> 00:21:23,420
for FitImage. So we have the images device tree or node with all the different binaries

357
00:21:23,420 --> 00:21:31,440
where you can find them, where you can load your binaries, which key to use to sign each

358
00:21:31,440 --> 00:21:38,440
of the binaries. And you have the configurations on the right. And there is an important line

359
00:21:38,440 --> 00:21:42,200
which is default equals conf at one.

360
00:21:42,200 --> 00:21:46,180
And this will be important in the next upcoming slides.

361
00:21:46,180 --> 00:21:50,480
And you can see that the conf at one has kernel

362
00:21:50,480 --> 00:21:52,100
is equal to kernel at one.

363
00:21:52,100 --> 00:21:54,460
So it will be using the first kernel

364
00:21:54,460 --> 00:21:58,680
and the device reads, which is also the first one.

365
00:21:58,680 --> 00:22:03,780
So there is a little bit of tricks

366
00:22:03,780 --> 00:22:07,020
to do before compiling your uBoot binary.

367
00:22:07,020 --> 00:22:13,180
you have to actually compile your device which contains

368
00:22:13,180 --> 00:22:18,800
the embedded public key outside of your UBoot source

369
00:22:18,800 --> 00:22:20,200
repository.

370
00:22:20,200 --> 00:22:24,760
And this is because we need to use

371
00:22:24,760 --> 00:22:26,900
MKMet, which is a tool from UBoot,

372
00:22:26,900 --> 00:22:29,780
to actually embed the public key before we are going

373
00:22:29,780 --> 00:22:32,580
to build the UBoot binary.

374
00:22:32,580 --> 00:22:37,380
So you use DTC to do the first compilation

375
00:22:37,380 --> 00:22:40,360
of the uBoot public key, the DTB.

376
00:22:40,360 --> 00:22:43,320
Then you compile all the tools from uBoot.

377
00:22:43,320 --> 00:22:47,200
You run MKImage to actually build the fit image

378
00:22:47,200 --> 00:22:49,480
and at the same time embed the public key

379
00:22:49,480 --> 00:22:52,240
within the device tree.

380
00:22:52,240 --> 00:22:53,740
And then you compile the uBoot binary

381
00:22:53,740 --> 00:22:55,880
using the external device tree blob, which

382
00:22:55,880 --> 00:23:00,080
is called uBoot underscore public key.

383
00:23:00,080 --> 00:23:03,020
These are the, well, the configuration

384
00:23:03,020 --> 00:23:08,240
you have to put to enable the fit image signature

385
00:23:08,240 --> 00:23:12,120
verification in U-boot.

386
00:23:12,120 --> 00:23:16,360
They say config of control is for embedding, actually,

387
00:23:16,360 --> 00:23:21,160
using, actually, the device support in U-boot.

388
00:23:21,160 --> 00:23:24,060
And the first two items are NXP-specific.

389
00:23:24,060 --> 00:23:33,040
So this is actually what it looks like to boot a fit image

390
00:23:33,040 --> 00:23:35,660
with all the verification steps, et cetera.

391
00:23:35,660 --> 00:23:40,200
So provided you have a fit image loaded at 0x15, let's say,

392
00:23:40,200 --> 00:23:44,920
so you do boot m 0x15 pound conf at 1.

393
00:23:44,920 --> 00:23:50,440
But since the default in the configurations node

394
00:23:50,440 --> 00:23:52,700
in the source file of your fit image,

395
00:23:52,700 --> 00:23:55,800
you know that conf at 1 is the default one.

396
00:23:55,800 --> 00:23:59,200
So you can actually just keep the part after the pound.

397
00:23:59,200 --> 00:24:02,580
So bootem XORX 15 is fine.

398
00:24:02,580 --> 00:24:05,200
You can see that it's using conf at 1.

399
00:24:05,200 --> 00:24:08,860
It's trying to load the kernel at 1.

400
00:24:08,860 --> 00:24:12,160
It does all of those things and verify the hash integrity,

401
00:24:12,160 --> 00:24:13,620
which says, well, it's OK.

402
00:24:13,620 --> 00:24:19,860
So let's get to the next binary in the fit image,

403
00:24:19,860 --> 00:24:22,260
which is the .

404
00:24:22,260 --> 00:24:26,880
It checks it, well, it's fine, so let's get started with the kernel.

405
00:24:26,880 --> 00:24:34,800
But then, someone touched the device and, well, hopefully, it will get it and stops

406
00:24:34,800 --> 00:24:35,800
the boot.

407
00:24:35,800 --> 00:24:39,780
And that's what happened in the lower part of this slide.

408
00:24:39,780 --> 00:24:41,220
It's verifying the hash integrity.

409
00:24:41,220 --> 00:24:43,380
They say, no, I cannot verify it.

410
00:24:43,380 --> 00:24:50,000
So well, I'm stopping there, and I'm crashing you, but you shouldn't stop, stop here.

411
00:24:50,000 --> 00:24:55,540
So I think that was it for the part up to the root file system.

412
00:24:55,540 --> 00:25:01,060
So now we will present her part on the root file system.

413
00:25:01,060 --> 00:25:06,720
So it's the final part of the chain of trust.

414
00:25:06,720 --> 00:25:14,120
To have a verified root file system, we have chosen the following solution.

415
00:25:14,120 --> 00:25:19,280
You can have other kind of solution, but this is what we choose.

416
00:25:19,280 --> 00:25:24,700
So first of all, we wanted to have an unalterable file system.

417
00:25:24,700 --> 00:25:31,920
So we choose to have a read-only file system, so it's impossible to modify it.

418
00:25:31,920 --> 00:25:38,480
So we use a SquashFS image, which is a type for read-only file system.

419
00:25:38,480 --> 00:25:44,520
So this part is not really part of the secure board process, but it was important for us

420
00:25:44,520 --> 00:25:47,700
and for our customer.

421
00:25:47,700 --> 00:25:55,860
So the authentication of the root file system, so it's really the part of the chain of trust,

422
00:25:55,860 --> 00:25:57,680
we use DMVarity.

423
00:25:57,680 --> 00:26:03,660
So DMVarity, it's an infrastructure to check if the root file system is the one we are

424
00:26:03,660 --> 00:26:05,960
expecting to have.

425
00:26:05,960 --> 00:26:10,760
So in fact, it's the authentication of the SquashFS image.

426
00:26:10,760 --> 00:26:17,140
And DMVarity needs some user space application to authenticate the system.

427
00:26:17,140 --> 00:26:22,840
And so you need to have these tools available.

428
00:26:22,840 --> 00:26:29,100
So that's why we choose to have init1fs as a first file system.

429
00:26:29,100 --> 00:26:38,420
And in this init1fs, you will have the application tools to perform the authentication of the

430
00:26:38,420 --> 00:26:41,500
root file system.

431
00:26:41,500 --> 00:26:44,240
The init1fs is built in the kernel.

432
00:26:44,240 --> 00:26:53,200
So thanks to that, it doesn't break the chain of trust because, as Compton told you before,

433
00:26:53,200 --> 00:27:00,840
the kernel is already in the chain of trust using the fit image.

434
00:27:00,840 --> 00:27:02,220
So now DMVVT.

435
00:27:02,220 --> 00:27:07,220
So DM, it stands for device mapper.

436
00:27:07,220 --> 00:27:14,500
It's an infrastructure in the Linux kernel to create virtual layers on block devices.

437
00:27:14,500 --> 00:27:22,700
So device mapper Verity, it provides integrity checking of block devices using kernel crypto

438
00:27:22,700 --> 00:27:23,700
API.

439
00:27:23,700 --> 00:27:33,320
Then Verity could hash the whole block device and then compare the hash with an expected

440
00:27:33,320 --> 00:27:35,480
hash, but it's not doing that.

441
00:27:35,480 --> 00:27:38,780
Otherwise, the boot time will be very, very long.

442
00:27:38,780 --> 00:27:44,620
So instead of that, it's use cryptographic hash tree.

443
00:27:44,620 --> 00:27:49,160
And your block device will be split in small block,

444
00:27:49,160 --> 00:27:52,760
and each block will have its hash.

445
00:27:52,760 --> 00:27:57,780
And it will create a hash tree until the last hash,

446
00:27:57,780 --> 00:27:59,460
which is called root hash.

447
00:27:59,460 --> 00:28:04,220
we will use this root hash in the authentication mechanism.

448
00:28:04,220 --> 00:28:08,080
So we will see it in the next slides.

449
00:28:08,080 --> 00:28:13,740
And this block are hashed only on access.

450
00:28:13,740 --> 00:28:18,120
So instead of having a longer boot time,

451
00:28:18,120 --> 00:28:26,080
we will just have a smaller, long access on a block device.

452
00:28:26,080 --> 00:28:31,880
And as I said before, DMVT needs some user space application.

453
00:28:31,880 --> 00:28:35,860
So CryptSetup package provides different tools,

454
00:28:35,860 --> 00:28:37,480
such as VeritySetup.

455
00:28:37,480 --> 00:28:39,320
So we'll see in the next slide.

456
00:28:39,320 --> 00:28:44,400
VeritySetup is a tool to create the hash tree

457
00:28:44,400 --> 00:28:46,480
and also to authenticate the device.

458
00:28:49,780 --> 00:28:55,740
So DMVT in our case, so we have our kernel.

459
00:28:55,740 --> 00:29:00,500
DemVarity has some configuration in the kernel,

460
00:29:00,500 --> 00:29:03,000
so you need to enable this configuration

461
00:29:03,000 --> 00:29:05,740
to have DemVarity.

462
00:29:05,740 --> 00:29:08,380
And as I said before, we have a NeetromFS,

463
00:29:08,380 --> 00:29:13,500
and we created a script that will use a Verity Setup tool

464
00:29:13,500 --> 00:29:17,900
to perform the verification on our block device.

465
00:29:17,900 --> 00:29:21,100
For our use case, we use UBI block device,

466
00:29:21,100 --> 00:29:24,420
but it can be other kind of block device.

467
00:29:25,300 --> 00:29:28,440
If the verification is successful,

468
00:29:28,440 --> 00:29:32,680
then you can use mount command, for example,

469
00:29:32,680 --> 00:29:37,680
to have access of your squashfs that you verified.

470
00:29:37,680 --> 00:29:39,180
And if it's not the case,

471
00:29:39,180 --> 00:29:43,100
then it will fail to mount the squashfs

472
00:29:43,100 --> 00:29:46,080
and it will stop the init here.

473
00:29:49,600 --> 00:29:53,500
So now let's see how to create the hash tree

474
00:29:53,500 --> 00:29:58,000
that we have seen in previous slide.

475
00:29:58,000 --> 00:30:05,960
So Verity Setup can create the hash tree on device or images.

476
00:30:05,960 --> 00:30:09,060
In this example, it's on images, but you

477
00:30:09,060 --> 00:30:11,780
can do it on device node.

478
00:30:11,780 --> 00:30:14,120
So we have our SquashFS image.

479
00:30:14,120 --> 00:30:18,500
We perform Verity Setup format command.

480
00:30:18,500 --> 00:30:22,560
The resulting image is a hash image,

481
00:30:22,560 --> 00:30:24,800
and it's containing the hash tree

482
00:30:24,800 --> 00:30:28,920
that we will use to authenticate the device.

483
00:30:28,920 --> 00:30:33,920
And it also prints on your screen the root hash.

484
00:30:33,920 --> 00:30:36,300
And you need to keep it,

485
00:30:36,300 --> 00:30:39,060
because we will use it to authenticate the device

486
00:30:39,060 --> 00:30:42,200
to be sure that the SquashFS image

487
00:30:42,200 --> 00:30:46,160
that we've created the hash tree right now,

488
00:30:46,160 --> 00:30:49,600
it's the correct one that we want to authenticate.

489
00:30:49,600 --> 00:31:00,620
So by default, the DemVerity and VeritySetup expect to have a block device with data in

490
00:31:00,620 --> 00:31:05,080
there and another block device with the hash tree.

491
00:31:05,080 --> 00:31:13,520
So the command used is VeritySetup format, then the data device, and the hash device.

492
00:31:13,520 --> 00:31:18,100
But in fact, in our use case, we wanted to have only one device.

493
00:31:18,100 --> 00:31:22,500
So our block device will have the SquashFS image

494
00:31:22,500 --> 00:31:26,340
and also it will contain the hash tree.

495
00:31:27,600 --> 00:31:29,980
For that, we can contain the hash image

496
00:31:29,980 --> 00:31:34,380
at the end of the SquashFS image

497
00:31:34,380 --> 00:31:39,380
to have at the end final SquashFS containing also the hash tree.

498
00:31:43,060 --> 00:31:45,400
To do that, Verity Setup tool

499
00:31:45,400 --> 00:31:50,400
as an option, hash offset,

500
00:31:52,120 --> 00:31:57,120
and to say how to locate the hash area

501
00:31:58,400 --> 00:32:01,160
in the same device or image.

502
00:32:01,160 --> 00:32:05,900
So here's the example of the command,

503
00:32:05,900 --> 00:32:09,660
so verity setup format, then we set the offset,

504
00:32:09,660 --> 00:32:11,740
so where we can find the hash tree

505
00:32:11,740 --> 00:32:14,920
in the same device or image.

506
00:32:16,400 --> 00:32:18,200
Currently, in our example,

507
00:32:19,380 --> 00:32:24,380
because the hash tree is at the end of the SquashFS image,

508
00:32:24,380 --> 00:32:28,300
the offset is the size of the SquashFS image.

509
00:32:29,580 --> 00:32:31,320
And then the data device,

510
00:32:31,320 --> 00:32:34,580
so in our use case, it's the SquashFS image.

511
00:32:34,580 --> 00:32:39,400
And the final result, so it's the SquashFS image

512
00:32:39,400 --> 00:32:43,000
and also the hash image.

513
00:32:45,380 --> 00:32:48,160
So now we have created our hash tree.

514
00:32:48,160 --> 00:32:50,740
We have the root hash,

515
00:32:50,740 --> 00:32:54,260
and we will see how to authenticate the device.

516
00:32:55,960 --> 00:32:58,200
So now we are on our platform.

517
00:32:58,200 --> 00:33:01,800
We have flashed all the different images,

518
00:33:01,800 --> 00:33:05,480
and we have our block device

519
00:33:05,480 --> 00:33:09,400
that contains the SquashFS and the hash image.

520
00:33:09,400 --> 00:33:12,260
We use Verity Setup create command

521
00:33:12,260 --> 00:33:17,260
that we need the offset to know where to find the hash tree

522
00:33:17,680 --> 00:33:19,360
and also the root hash.

523
00:33:20,600 --> 00:33:23,600
If the hash corresponds to the one

524
00:33:23,600 --> 00:33:26,640
that is available in the hash tree,

525
00:33:26,640 --> 00:33:31,640
then you have a mapper device on the SquashFS verified.

526
00:33:31,640 --> 00:33:38,280
So at the end, you will have a slash dev, slash mapper, slash something.

527
00:33:38,280 --> 00:33:45,920
And on this device mapper, you can use the standard mount command to then have access

528
00:33:45,920 --> 00:33:49,780
to your verified SquashFS.

529
00:33:49,780 --> 00:33:59,300
So the command you use is a very tight setup create, then the name of the device mapper.

530
00:33:59,300 --> 00:34:02,260
So in our example, it's a root file system.

531
00:34:02,260 --> 00:34:04,140
It can be another one.

532
00:34:06,000 --> 00:34:11,000
Again, we use the hash offset option of Verity Setup

533
00:34:12,140 --> 00:34:17,140
to say where to locate the hash tree in the device block.

534
00:34:21,380 --> 00:34:24,960
Then there is a data device and the hash device.

535
00:34:24,960 --> 00:34:27,960
In our use case, it's the same one.

536
00:34:27,960 --> 00:34:31,080
and finally, the root hash.

537
00:34:32,600 --> 00:34:35,920
So because we use the same block device

538
00:34:35,920 --> 00:34:40,920
and it contains both data and hash image,

539
00:34:41,280 --> 00:34:44,960
we use UBI block zero twice.

540
00:34:44,960 --> 00:34:48,000
Of course, if you use a default mechanism,

541
00:34:48,000 --> 00:34:53,000
so if you have, for example, the data on UBI block zero

542
00:34:53,600 --> 00:34:56,900
and hash three on UBI block one,

543
00:34:56,900 --> 00:35:00,580
then you don't use hash offset parameters,

544
00:35:00,580 --> 00:35:03,380
and you will have block zero and block one

545
00:35:03,380 --> 00:35:06,120
in your command varity setup create.

546
00:35:10,080 --> 00:35:14,140
Yeah, so here you can see that we have the offset

547
00:35:14,140 --> 00:35:16,660
and the hash parameters,

548
00:35:16,660 --> 00:35:21,660
and we need a way to have it available in the kernel.

549
00:35:22,160 --> 00:35:24,700
So in fact, we use both args,

550
00:35:25,600 --> 00:35:29,700
And for that, we created a U-boot environment script.

551
00:35:31,260 --> 00:35:34,740
But this new component can be attacked.

552
00:35:34,740 --> 00:35:37,880
So to not again break the chain of trust,

553
00:35:37,880 --> 00:35:40,380
we add it in the fit image.

554
00:35:40,380 --> 00:35:44,320
So in the fit image, we have the U-boot environment script

555
00:35:44,320 --> 00:35:46,260
with the hash,

556
00:35:48,140 --> 00:35:52,020
which has the signature of the hash of the binary.

557
00:35:52,020 --> 00:35:56,720
Then u-boot checks the signature of this u-boot environment

558
00:35:56,720 --> 00:35:58,000
script.

559
00:35:58,000 --> 00:36:04,260
If the signature is matching, then it can source this script.

560
00:36:04,260 --> 00:36:08,500
And in this script, we have some variables

561
00:36:08,500 --> 00:36:12,940
to retrieve the offset and the hash.

562
00:36:12,940 --> 00:36:16,520
And also, of course, it sets the boot hash.

563
00:36:16,520 --> 00:36:21,900
Thanks to that, we can retrieve it in the kernel.

564
00:36:21,900 --> 00:36:25,300
So here, it's the final mechanism.

565
00:36:25,300 --> 00:36:27,320
So as I said before, we have uBoot.

566
00:36:27,320 --> 00:36:31,760
It checks the signature of the uBoot environment script.

567
00:36:31,760 --> 00:36:36,120
If it's not the correct one, it will fail to source it.

568
00:36:36,120 --> 00:36:39,460
If it's the correct one, it will source the script.

569
00:36:39,460 --> 00:36:41,500
Thanks to that, in the boot args,

570
00:36:41,500 --> 00:36:45,400
we will have the offset and the hash.

571
00:36:45,400 --> 00:36:50,180
Then we have our kernel with DMVVT enabled, our init1FS

572
00:36:50,180 --> 00:36:54,760
with the init script that will use the Verity Setup command

573
00:36:54,760 --> 00:36:59,540
to do the verification on the UBI block device.

574
00:36:59,540 --> 00:37:02,900
Of course, as we have seen before, Verity Setup command

575
00:37:02,900 --> 00:37:05,280
needs the hash and the offset.

576
00:37:05,280 --> 00:37:08,860
So thanks to the boot args, it can retrieve it.

577
00:37:08,860 --> 00:37:12,020
And if the verification is successful,

578
00:37:12,020 --> 00:37:18,060
then you have a device mapper, and you can do a switch route.

579
00:37:18,060 --> 00:37:22,120
So in our use case, we use switch routes instead of mount

580
00:37:22,120 --> 00:37:26,040
because we wanted to switch the root file system

581
00:37:26,040 --> 00:37:29,880
from the init1fs to the verified squashfs.

582
00:37:29,880 --> 00:37:32,820
Otherwise, our system will always

583
00:37:32,820 --> 00:37:35,500
be on the init1fs, and we don't want that.

584
00:37:38,380 --> 00:37:41,840
And to complete the chain of trust,

585
00:37:41,840 --> 00:37:46,460
so as we added a new component, so use a U-boot environment

586
00:37:46,460 --> 00:37:49,440
script, for example, we add it in the fit image

587
00:37:49,440 --> 00:37:51,600
to not break the chain of trust.

588
00:37:55,040 --> 00:37:56,420
Thanks, Milan.

589
00:37:56,420 --> 00:38:03,100
So now we have actually completed our chain of trust.

590
00:38:03,100 --> 00:38:07,900
So basically, now to summarize what

591
00:38:07,900 --> 00:38:11,580
has been done yet until now.

592
00:38:11,580 --> 00:38:18,360
So in the ROM code, you have up to four hash of public keys,

593
00:38:18,360 --> 00:38:20,740
which are used to verify the public key which

594
00:38:20,740 --> 00:38:24,640
is embedded in the binary of the bootloader.

595
00:38:24,640 --> 00:38:27,180
Then when it's verified, the public key,

596
00:38:27,180 --> 00:38:28,880
then you can use it to verify, actually,

597
00:38:28,880 --> 00:38:30,620
the signature of the bootloader.

598
00:38:30,620 --> 00:38:32,880
Once that's done, you have another public key

599
00:38:32,880 --> 00:38:35,300
in the uBoot binary, which is used

600
00:38:35,300 --> 00:38:38,380
to verify the signature of the kernel and the DTB,

601
00:38:38,380 --> 00:38:40,380
Well, basically the fit image, the whole fit image

602
00:38:40,380 --> 00:38:44,920
with the UBoot script presented just the slides before.

603
00:38:44,920 --> 00:38:46,960
And once that's done, you have the digest

604
00:38:46,960 --> 00:38:49,720
within the fit image, which is used to verify

605
00:38:49,720 --> 00:38:52,600
and authenticate the rootFS.

606
00:38:52,600 --> 00:38:55,920
So yeah, basically that's done.

607
00:38:55,920 --> 00:38:59,220
But now we really want some kind of, I would say,

608
00:38:59,220 --> 00:39:02,400
distribution as a rootFS.

609
00:39:02,400 --> 00:39:04,720
And we don't want to, well, do it manually.

610
00:39:04,720 --> 00:39:08,860
We can use build root, Yocto, whatever build system you want.

611
00:39:08,860 --> 00:39:11,020
But in our case, it was Yocto.

612
00:39:11,020 --> 00:39:13,460
But Yocto has a really specific way

613
00:39:13,460 --> 00:39:17,060
to create fit images.

614
00:39:17,060 --> 00:39:20,500
And currently, it's the kernel recipe

615
00:39:20,500 --> 00:39:24,380
that actually inherits the kernel fit image class.

616
00:39:24,380 --> 00:39:31,760
And that means that, well, it's done before the root

617
00:39:31,760 --> 00:39:33,180
has been created.

618
00:39:33,180 --> 00:39:35,880
And I think the point is that actually people

619
00:39:35,880 --> 00:39:39,320
want to have the fit image, the kernel, whatever,

620
00:39:39,320 --> 00:39:42,900
within the slash boot of the rootFS.

621
00:39:42,900 --> 00:39:48,680
But our script needs the rootFS to be actually compiled, built,

622
00:39:48,680 --> 00:39:53,800
so that we can compute the hash and put it in the U-boot script.

623
00:39:53,800 --> 00:39:58,680
But, well, we want it in the fit image, which

624
00:39:58,680 --> 00:40:01,620
is created before the rootFS.

625
00:40:01,620 --> 00:40:04,520
So basically, that's how we end up with a dependency loop

626
00:40:04,520 --> 00:40:07,540
in Yocto, and that's really hard to debug.

627
00:40:07,540 --> 00:40:10,740
So while we throw away the kernel feature image class

628
00:40:10,740 --> 00:40:13,920
and wrote a new image and class recipes

629
00:40:13,920 --> 00:40:16,800
to work around this issue, it's really, I would say,

630
00:40:16,800 --> 00:40:20,440
the level of draft or proof of concept.

631
00:40:20,440 --> 00:40:24,320
But that works, so that was it for us.

632
00:40:24,320 --> 00:40:31,400
So well, it was a very specific example we presented today.

633
00:40:31,400 --> 00:40:35,180
to have only read-only file system.

634
00:40:35,180 --> 00:40:39,780
But you definitely may want to have some read-only file

635
00:40:39,780 --> 00:40:44,960
system besides your read-only file system.

636
00:40:44,960 --> 00:40:47,780
So if it stores not critical data,

637
00:40:47,780 --> 00:40:53,340
well, just mount it alongside your read-only file system.

638
00:40:53,340 --> 00:40:54,020
That's fine.

639
00:40:54,020 --> 00:40:56,360
You can store blogs, user data.

640
00:40:56,360 --> 00:40:57,640
Well, you don't care.

641
00:40:57,640 --> 00:41:02,260
Well, if it's critical, well, we haven't really looked at it.

642
00:41:02,260 --> 00:41:06,100
But a hint would be to look at IMA EVM.

643
00:41:06,100 --> 00:41:09,880
We have provided a bit of toolings to look at it.

